 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as text, with syntax highlighting. Use the :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method, which takes the JSON text and optional ``indent`` (number of spaces, default 4) and ``highlight`` (default ``True``) keyword arguments::
+
+    from rich.console import Console
+
+    console = Console()
+    console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The JSON is parsed first, so invalid JSON raises the parser's error and nothing is printed.
+
+The same output is available as a renderable, :class:`~rich.json.JSON`, which you can pass to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. You can also use the package-level :func:`rich.print_json` function, which prints through the global console::
+
+    from rich import print_json
+
+    print_json('{"name": "Rich"}', indent=2)
+
+JSON output is not wrapped: the renderable disables word wrapping. Printing with default settings still crops lines wider than the console, so to keep long lines whole, enable soft wrapping with ``soft_wrap=True``.
+
+To pretty print a JSON file from the command line, run the json module with the path to the file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
